issues.issue:
"Nothing personal" can't be literally true for any network SDK. The receiving server sees IP addresses, and reports usually carry an install ID. Under GDPR both are personal data, so the owner becomes a controller who needs a processor agreement. The docs don't specify:
- separate switches for crash reports and usage analytics;
- both off by default;
- the SDK starting only after consent (Sentry Android starts itself from a ContentProvider unless that is disabled);
- withdrawal in Settings;
- vendor and hosting region, and retention;
- what the privacy policy and the Data safety form will say.

"Money" calls onionhoney's written s.7 permission a blocker for the first paid feature ("Being verified"). The owner to-do still reads "Optional: write to onionhoney ... and ask for an explicit licence statement". Upstream has been dormant since 2025-07-27, so there may be only one chance to ask. A letter that asks only for a licence statement would leave several things open:
- Play Billing and any other closed Play library (compliance-1);
- the question of Play's terms of service against the GPL;
- whether the listing may credit them by name.

issues.fix:
Turn the to-do into one letter, not optional while paid extras stay planned, asking onionhoney (they) for:
1. an explicit statement that roux-trainers is GPL-3.0-only;
2. a s.7 additional permission to link Google Play libraries (Billing and other com.google.android.play or gms artifacts) with the ported code, worded like SPDX GPL-3.0-linking-exception minus the source sentence;
3. an app-store permission in Terpoir's style, so Play's terms don't count as further restrictions;
4. consent to name "Roux Trainers"/onionhoney in the listing's credit line.
Record the reply in the repo, e.g. in LICENSING.md.

Before the first external pull request:
- add CONTRIBUTING.md with a DCO sign-off, checked in CI;
- state that contributions are GPL-3.0-only and that each contributor also grants the additional permissions in LICENSING.md (Google Play libraries, app stores) for their part;
- add a templates/README that states the templates' licence;
- use the same text as Weblate's contributor agreement;
- make HANDOFF and scope agree on when Weblate starts.

issues.issue:
The About/Licences screen is the only GPL duty in scope.md. The others appear only in the research note:
- SPDX header and copyright lines on every file, keeping onionhoney's line on ported files;
- a dated modification notice on each ported file, naming the upstream path and commit;
- a tag and source archive for every published build;
- Corresponding Source that includes the CI workflow and any pruning-table generator;
- the licence text viewable in the app;
- Apache-2.0 notices (AndroidX, Material Symbols) in the Licences screen;
- no EULA or ToS terms.
Tickets written from HANDOFF and scope won't carry them.

issues.fix:
Put a "GPL duties in every build" checklist in HANDOFF "Decided" (or in LICENSING.md) and make it part of step 1's acceptance criteria:
- an SPDX check in CI (e.g. REUSE lint);
- a port-notice template: "Ported to Kotlin from onionhoney/roux-trainers src/lib/X.tsx @0129460 on <date>";
- a release workflow that attaches the source archive and names the tag;
- the About screen showing the versionName with the commit SHA for `dev` builds.
